    "meta": {
        "modules": [
            {
                "module_name": "smsmms",
                "module_filename": "smsmms.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_sms_mms",
                        "tablename": "get_sms_mms",
                        "name": "SMS Messages",
                        "description": "SMS messages from mmssms.db (incl. LG extended types and Samsung spam_sms)",
                        "author": "@ydkhatri",
                        "created_date": "2020-03-10",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-09-02",
                        "notes": "SMS date values are in milliseconds and MMS pdu.date values are in seconds; the provider's own union query multiplies pdu.date by 1000 to line the two up. Reference: AOSP, 'MmsSmsProvider.java, getConversations and buildConversationQuery', https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/providers/TelephonyProvider/+/bca387f553a4493c88e24455172225fd1049c91f/src/com/android/providers/telephony/MmsSmsProvider.java. The LG extended sms.type values 7, 8 and 19 are labelled Blocked Number, Scheduled Send and Broadcast Alert; that mapping is not vendor-documented and no source for it is cited, so the labels are unverified. Any other value is reported as stored.",
                        "category": "SMS & MMS"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_sms_mms_combined",
                        "tablename": "get_sms_mms_combined",
                        "name": "SMS and MMS Messages",
                        "description": "SMS and MMS messages from mmssms.db in one table, with a Message Type column stating which each row is. Complements the separate SMS Messages and MMS Messages artifacts rather than replacing them.",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Claude",
                        "created_date": "2026-09-02",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-09-02",
                        "notes": "The rows of the SMS Messages and MMS Messages artifacts in one table, sorted by Date, so a thread that carries both transports reads in one place. Both tables key on the same thread_id and the provider itself tells the two apart in its unified queries with a transport_type discriminator column. Reference: AOSP, 'Telephony.MmsSms.TYPE_DISCRIMINATOR_COLUMN', https://developer.android.com/reference/android/provider/Telephony.MmsSms#TYPE_DISCRIMINATOR_COLUMN. Message Type is SMS or MMS, and Source Table names the table each row was read from (sms, spam_sms or pdu); both are uniform on an image holding only one transport. Address is sms.address for SMS rows (the other party, per Telephony.TextBasedSmsColumns.ADDRESS) and the From (PduHeaders.FROM, 0x89) addr entry for MMS rows. To Address, Cc and Bcc are filled for MMS rows only; Contact ID, Service Center and Error Code are filled for SMS rows only. MMS rows are one per non-SMIL part, so a multi-part MMS repeats its MSG ID, and MSG ID is sms._id or pdu._id, unique only together with Message Type. Media renders the part file when it is in the extraction, matched on the part's recorded _data path (storage class, Android user, package and file name) rather than on the file name alone, and is blank for SMS rows, for text parts, and for parts whose file is not in the image (the part's stored path is then shown in Body). Direction maps sms.type and pdu.msg_box through the same words from the AOSP constants (Received, Sent, Draft, Outbox, Failed; Queued exists for SMS only), with the LG extended sms.type values handled as in the SMS Messages artifact. A pdu.msg_box of 0 is shown as 'All messages' and any other msg_box value is shown as stored. References: AOSP, 'Telephony.TextBasedSmsColumns MESSAGE_TYPE constants', https://developer.android.com/reference/android/provider/Telephony.TextBasedSmsColumns; AOSP, 'Telephony.BaseMmsColumns MESSAGE_BOX constants', https://developer.android.com/reference/android/provider/Telephony.BaseMmsColumns. SMS date values are in milliseconds and MMS pdu.date values are in seconds; the provider's own union query multiplies pdu.date by 1000 to line the two up. Reference: AOSP, 'MmsSmsProvider.java, getConversations and buildConversationQuery', https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/providers/TelephonyProvider/+/bca387f553a4493c88e24455172225fd1049c91f/src/com/android/providers/telephony/MmsSmsProvider.java",
                        "category": "SMS & MMS"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "WhatsApp",
                "module_filename": "WhatsApp.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_whatsapp_contacts",
                        "tablename": "get_whatsapp_contacts",
                        "name": "WhatsApp - Contacts",
                        "description": "Rows of WhatsApp's wa.db wa_contacts table (name columns, wa_name, jid, number and status text), excluding channel (@newsletter) and status@broadcast jids",
                        "author": "@abrignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2021-03-11",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-09-27",
                        "notes": "Reads the first wa.db matched and reports its wa_contacts rows, except rows whose jid ends in @newsletter, the jid form of the chats msgstore.db's newsletter table describes, and the row whose jid is status@broadcast. When any rows are left out, their number is written to the run log. On the 12 tested images the rows left out numbered 791 (781 @newsletter, 10 status@broadcast); none held a given name, family name, display name or number, so each would have shown its jid as Name and JID, with no Number. wa.db holds more @newsletter jids than msgstore.db's newsletter table holds channels: 275 against 10 channels on kevin_pocox7_a15, 191 against 5 on russell_a14, and 255 on anne_a15, whose newsletter table is empty. The @newsletter jids with no chat in msgstore.db appeared in no other TEXT column of wa.db or msgstore.db on any tested image (full-text index tables were not searched). What those rows record was not established, and an @newsletter jid in wa.db does not show that a channel was followed. Name is given_name and family_name when either is not null, otherwise display_name, otherwise the jid; 1,722 of the 1,809 reported rows across the tested images showed the jid as Name. WhatsApp Name is the wa_name column as stored, and it is never used to fill Name. It was set on 230 of the 1,809 rows, 210 of them rows whose Name was the jid; on the 20 rows where both it and one of given_name, family_name or display_name were set, it differed from Name on 15. In 20 pairs of tested images, one device's own account appeared in the other device's wa_contacts; wa_name was set in 10 of them, and in all 10 it equalled the push_name value in the first device's own WhatsApp preferences (startup_prefs.xml, or com.whatsapp_preferences_light.xml on pixel3_a11 and pixel3_a12). A published description of wa_name is 'WhatsApp name of the contact (as set in their profile)'. Reference: Igor Mikhailov, 'WhatsApp in Plain Sight: Where and How You Can Collect Forensic Artifacts', https://www.group-ib.com/blog/whatsapp-forensic-artifacts/. When wa_name was written, and whether it follows later changes to the contact's profile name, was not established. Number is the number column as stored, with no fallback to the jid; it was empty on 1,731 of the 1,809 rows. Status Text is the status column as stored, and Status Timestamp is status_timestamp read as milliseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC, shown blank where the stored value is 0. Status Text was set on 730 of the 1,809 rows and Status Timestamp on 531, all of them rows with a Status Text; the other 199 rows with a Status Text stored a timestamp of 0. Of the 20 pairs of tested images above, status was set in 15; in the 11 of those whose first device stores a my_current_status value in its own com.whatsapp_preferences_light.xml, status equalled that value in all 11. Where one contact carried the same Status Text on two different tested phones with a timestamp on both (4 contacts), Status Timestamp was identical on both. What event Status Timestamp marks was not established. A published description of status is 'Text in the status line of the contact'. Reference: Igor Mikhailov, 'WhatsApp in Plain Sight: Where and How You Can Collect Forensic Artifacts', https://www.group-ib.com/blog/whatsapp-forensic-artifacts/. Rows whose jid ends in @g.us, @lid or @bot are reported as stored.",
                        "category": "WhatsApp"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_whatsapp_call_logs",
                        "tablename": "get_whatsapp_call_logs",
                        "name": "WhatsApp - Call Logs",
                        "description": "WhatsApp call logs (msgstore.db)",
                        "author": "@abrignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2021-03-11",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "A call whose jid is a LID jid (...@lid) is matched to wa.db contacts through msgstore.db jid_map when that table exists. Calls that match no contact are still reported. With no wa.db every call is still reported and an incoming call's Caller is shown by jid; run on copies of the 12 listed images' msgstore.db with wa.db left out, the artifact reported the same number of calls as with it. Call End Timestamp is not stored: it is the call's timestamp plus its duration read as seconds. On an outgoing call Caller reads Self and Caller JID is blank. For an incoming call that matches no contact, or whose contact has no WhatsApp name, Caller is shown by jid. The LID-keyed case and the no-contact case are exercised by a constructed test (admin/test/scripts/test_whatsapp_lid_contacts.py); whether any image listed in sample_data holds such a call is not stated here.",
                        "category": "WhatsApp"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_whatsapp_one_to_one_messages",
                        "tablename": "get_whatsapp_one_to_one_messages",
                        "name": "WhatsApp - One To One Messages",
                        "description": "WhatsApp messages in chats whose jid is not a group (@g.us) or channel (@newsletter) jid (modern msgstore.db schema)",
                        "author": "@abrignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2021-03-11",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "Rows are selected by the chat's jid: every message in a chat whose jid does not end in @g.us or @newsletter. g.us is the server name the third-party open-source client whatsmeow gives for group jids (GroupServer, https://github.com/tulir/whatsmeow/blob/8b41cfe6d9c487e17858cf00be40e951f575dbe8/types/jid.go#L24); no WhatsApp source for it is cited here. On the 12 listed images this selects the same rows as message.recipient_count = 0, which this artifact used before: every message in an @g.us chat stored a recipient_count of 1 or more, and every message in another chat stored 0. A chat keyed by any other jid form is reported here as stored; on the listed images the chats holding these rows were keyed by @s.whatsapp.net and @lid jids only. Message Type shows this module's label for message_type 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 9 and 16; other values are shown as stored and no source for the labels is cited here. A chat keyed by a LID jid (...@lid) is matched to wa.db contacts through msgstore.db jid_map when that table exists. Messages whose chat matches no contact are still reported. With no wa.db every message is still reported and the participant is shown by jid; run on copies of the 12 listed images' msgstore.db with wa.db left out, the artifact reported the same number of rows as with it. When no contact matches, or the contact has no WhatsApp name, the participant is shown by jid. Other Participant JID is the jid of the matched wa_contacts row, otherwise the jid that jid_map links the chat's LID jid to, otherwise the chat's jid; it is filled on incoming and outgoing rows. The conversation view groups rows by Other Participant JID and labels each conversation with Other Participant WA User Name, so a LID-keyed chat and the phone-number chat that jid_map links it to are shown as one conversation, and two chats whose contacts share a WhatsApp name are shown as two conversations. On the listed images 9 WhatsApp names were each shared by the chats of two @s.whatsapp.net jids (1 on hc_pixel8pro_a16, 1 on hc_pixel8pro_a17, 1 on kevin_pocox7_a15, 1 on russell_a14, 2 on sharon_a13, 3 on sharon_a14). On sharon_a14, 48 LID-keyed chats each had such a phone-number chat. Messages in channel (newsletter) chats are not reported here; WhatsApp - Channel Messages reports them.",
                        "category": "WhatsApp"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "smsClockGap",
                "module_filename": "smsClockGap.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_sms_clock_gap",
                        "tablename": "get_sms_clock_gap",
                        "name": "SMS Device Clock Gap",
                        "description": "Received SMS with the stored service-centre time (date_sent) beside the stored receive time (date), the difference in seconds and the app that wrote the row. A consistent difference across rows can indicate a device clock that read ahead of or behind the service centre; see the notes for limits.",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Claude",
                        "created_date": "2026-11-16",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-11-16",
                        "notes": "One row per received SMS (sms.type 1, MESSAGE_TYPE_INBOX) whose date and date_sent are both greater than zero, ordered by Service Centre Time within each database. In AOSP's reference Messaging app, date_sent is written from SmsMessage.getTimestampMillis(), the service-centre timestamp carried in the message, and date is the phone's clock when the app handled the message. If that clock reads before 18 Sep 2011, the app stores the service-centre time in date instead, so the gap is 0. References: https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/apps/Messaging/+/de315b762312dd1a5d2bbd16e62ef2bd123f61e5/src/com/android/messaging/sms/MmsUtils.java#1035 , https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/apps/Messaging/+/de315b762312dd1a5d2bbd16e62ef2bd123f61e5/src/com/android/messaging/sms/MmsUtils.java#1075 , https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/apps/Messaging/+/de315b762312dd1a5d2bbd16e62ef2bd123f61e5/src/com/android/messaging/receiver/SmsReceiver.java#208 , https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/1cdfff555f4a21f71ccc978290e2e212e2f8b168/telephony/java/com/android/internal/telephony/SmsMessageBase.java#214 , https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/1cdfff555f4a21f71ccc978290e2e212e2f8b168/core/java/android/provider/Telephony.java#144 . Creator (as stored) is the provider's creator column, documented as usually the package name of the app that wrote the row (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/1cdfff555f4a21f71ccc978290e2e212e2f8b168/core/java/android/provider/Telephony.java#280). Creator (as stored) held one value on all rows of each image checked (com.google.android.apps.messaging on all 53 rows of hc_pixel8pro_a16, com.samsung.android.messaging on all 58 rows of cookbook_a11); it is kept because it names the app whose behaviour applies. Google Messages and Samsung Messages are closed source. For Google Messages this reading rests on the test below. For Samsung Messages there is no test with a known clock state, and its rows are reported as stored. Gap (Seconds) is date minus date_sent. In the test below a clock set ahead gave a positive gap; a clock set behind was not tested and would be expected to give a negative one. The gap also includes any time the network held the message before delivery, and date_sent was a whole number of seconds on every row measured, so one row is not a clock offset. Compare the smallest gaps over a period. A step that persists across later rows is consistent with a clock change between the two messages where it starts. A gap near zero on a prompt delivery is consistent with a correct clock, not proof of one, and a gap of exactly 0 on every row means both columns hold one value and says nothing about the clock. Known data: on a Pixel 7a (Android 17, Google Messages) texting itself, the gap was 0.7 to 1.6 s (four messages) with the clock correct and 1200.2 and 1200.5 s (two messages) with the clock set 20 minutes ahead; that test read the SMS provider on the live phone, not an extraction. On the 18 corpus images listed in sample_data (1,944 rows; three pairs of images share rows, 1,801 distinct messages), the smallest gap per image was between -72.4 and 2.6 s and the median between 1.2 and 7.1 s, except cookbook_a11, where all 58 rows were written by Samsung Messages, Gap (Seconds) held 0.0 on all 58 rows and Service Centre Time (date_sent) and Received (Device Clock) were identical on all 58 rows, so that image allows no comparison. 301 rows had a gap over 60 s and 262 over one hour. 22 rows on 6 images (16 distinct messages) had negative gaps, from -72.4 to -0.004 s; 8 were below -1 s. Each mmssms.db found is listed as a source, including copies whose sms table held no rows. Sent messages are not reported: date_sent was 0 on every sent row of hc_pixel8pro_a16 and of the Pixel 7a test. Not read: MMS, Samsung's spam_sms table and RCS messages. Suggested by an examiner on a mailing list.",
                        "category": "SMS & MMS"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "calllog",
                "module_filename": "calllog.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_calllog",
                        "tablename": "get_calllog",
                        "name": "Call logs ",
                        "description": "Parses the call log (date, number, type, duration, the geocoded location stored for the number and transcription) from the contacts provider calllog.db, with the ICCID of the SIM account each call was placed or received on, where the extraction records the link.",
                        "author": "@abrignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex, Claude",
                        "created_date": "2020-03-02",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-11-16",
                        "notes": "The Android reference documents the DATE column as milliseconds since epoch and DURATION as seconds, and this parser decodes them that way, so the Call Date column is a converted millisecond timestamp and Duration in Secs is the stored value unchanged. The Type column decodes the AOSP CallLog.Calls codes 1 Incoming, 2 Outgoing, 3 Missed, 4 Voicemail, 5 Rejected, 6 Blocked and 7 Answered Externally. Any other stored value is shown as Unknown. Raw Call Type (as stored) preserves the source value beside the interpreted label. The same codes are applied to the Samsung contacts provider's calllog.db; whether Samsung stores other type values there is not established. That provider's calls table also holds SMS and MMS log rows, logtype 300 and 200, each carrying a telephony provider message id in messageid; in a calllog.db under com.samsung.android.providers.contacts they are left out here and reported by the Samsung Message Logs artifact. Rows with any other logtype (1000 and 100 on the images in sample_data; what 100 records is not established) or no logtype column are kept as calls. Versions of this artifact before 2026-11-16 listed those message rows as calls: 324 of 397 rows on sharon_a14. Reference: Android Developers, 'CallLog.Calls' API reference, https://developer.android.com/reference/android/provider/CallLog.Calls (read 2026-10-03)\n\nSIM ICCID and Phone Account Package. SIM ICCID is the ICCID of the SIM account the call was placed or received on, filled only where the extraction records the link (the stored phone account id equals a siminfo icc_id, equals one once a trailing F is removed from both, or is a subscription id under com.android.phone's TelephonyConnectionService that equals a siminfo _id); otherwise it is blank, and a blank means no recorded link was found, not that no SIM was used. Phone Account Package is the package part (before the slash) of subscription_component_name, the component of the account used to place or receive the call; In AOSP this is the ConnectionService that handles calls for the account (PhoneAccountHandle). The package is not necessarily the app the user dialled from. Phone Account Package held com.android.phone on all 73 rows of sharon_a14. A NULL is shown blank. How each ICCID was resolved, with the stored id, the full component and the first and last call per account, is in the Call Log Phone Accounts artifact.",
                        "category": "Call Logs"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_calllog_phone_accounts",
                        "tablename": "get_calllog_phone_accounts",
                        "name": "Call Log Phone Accounts",
                        "description": "One row per phone account in the contacts provider call log, with the ICCID of the SIM where the extraction records the link, how it was resolved, and the first and last call on the account.",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Claude",
                        "created_date": "2026-10-29",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-11-16",
                        "notes": "One row per distinct phone account recorded in the call log, keyed on the stored subscription_id and subscription_component_name, over every calllog.db read (all Android users together). First Call and Last Call are the earliest and latest call DATE for that account, converted from milliseconds since epoch to UTC. A call row with no DATE is counted but does not set either. Calls is the number of call rows for it. Rows are ordered by First Call. A change in the stored account id or component starts a new row. The same SIM can appear on more than one row: on pixel7a_a14 two rows resolve to one ICCID. CallLog.Calls keeps the call's phone account id in subscription_id and the account's component name in subscription_component_name. Both are shown as stored in Phone Account ID (as stored) and Phone Account Component; a NULL is shown blank, and call rows with no account recorded are counted together on one row. In AOSP the SIM phone account id is built by PhoneUtils.makePstnPhoneAccountHandleWithPrefix, which uses the full ICC serial number on the android12-release branch and the subscription id on the android13-release branch. The Android version alone does not tell which form a device stores: the pixel7a_a14 call log holds both forms across the same months, and why is not established. SIM ICCID is filled only from a link the extraction records, and ICCID Resolved Via names it. 'Stored value (matches siminfo)': the stored id equals an icc_id in the siminfo table of the telephony provider's telephony.db. 'Stored value, trailing F removed (matches siminfo)': where the stored id ends in F, it equals a siminfo icc_id once trailing F characters are removed from both values, and only one siminfo value matches that way. AOSP's getFullIccSerialNumber returns the ICC ID including hex digits; on pixel7a_a14 the ICCID-form stored ids carried a trailing F and the siminfo icc_id did not. 'siminfo subscription id': the component is com.android.phone's TelephonyConnectionService and the stored id equals a siminfo _id, which the Telephony provider defines as the subscription id. A subscription id under any other component, an id siminfo does not hold, an _id that maps to two different ICCIDs across telephony.db copies, and an extraction without telephony.db all leave SIM ICCID blank. A blank SIM ICCID means no recorded link was found, not that no SIM was used. On the corpora listed in sample_data every call under the TelephonyConnectionService component resolved. The Samsung contacts provider's SMS and MMS log rows (logtype 300 and 200) are not calls and are not counted here; with them left out, every call on the Samsung images in sample_data was under the TelephonyConnectionService component and every account row resolved a SIM ICCID. References: Telephony PhoneUtils.java at android12-release commit 7be6599cbdfecbe0c01438478492deb015846fa8 and android13-release commit 838fd09d2f8ddbce1330bfceae8ea24d85cc9182 (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/services/Telephony/+/7be6599cbdfecbe0c01438478492deb015846fa8/src/com/android/phone/PhoneUtils.java, https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/services/Telephony/+/838fd09d2f8ddbce1330bfceae8ea24d85cc9182/src/com/android/phone/PhoneUtils.java); frameworks/opt/telephony GsmCdmaPhone.java and uicc/IccRecords.java at android12-release commit 51302ef5e1a2f5c13b4db2650a1c52735ccca992; frameworks/base core/java/android/provider/CallLog.java and Telephony.java (SimInfo) at android14-release commit 6e47c7075b91983ae501114425ea25e6df7690c8 (all read 2026-10-29)",
                        "category": "Call Logs"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "usagestats",
                "module_filename": "usagestats.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_usagestats",
                        "tablename": "get_usagestats",
                        "name": "Usage Stats",
                        "description": "Package usage records, device configuration records and event-log entries read from the Android UsageStats XML and protobuf stores.",
                        "author": "Alexis Brignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2020-02-25",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-06",
                        "notes": "Usage Type names the record kind a row was read from: 'packages' and 'configurations' are per-interval aggregates written by the platform, 'event-log' rows are individually recorded events. Interval is set to daily, weekly, monthly or yearly when exactly one directory component beneath the selected store folder has that exact name. No match or multiple matching components leave Interval empty; host folders and file names are excluded. In the tested images the same record appears in more than one interval folder, so rows repeated across intervals are not necessarily separate occurrences. Event Type shows the name of the UsageEvents.Event constant whose value matches the stored numeric 'type' field; a value with no matching constant is shown as stored. The names for values the public reference does not list are the hidden constants in frameworks/base core/java/android/app/usage/UsageEvents.java at android-14.0.0_r1 (lines 90 to 344); other Android releases were not compared. The constant name is the platform's label for a recorded transition and does not by itself establish what a person did: ACTIVITY_RESUMED records an activity moving to the foreground, and SCREEN_INTERACTIVE records the screen entering an interactive state, which is a separate constant from KEYGUARD_SHOWN and KEYGUARD_HIDDEN and therefore does not record keyguard (lock) state. Timestamps are rendered in UTC. Each stored time is added to the interval file's numeric name, except that a negative stored value is used as its own magnitude; all four timestamp columns are converted the same way. The millisecond units in the column names come from the protobuf field names (last_time_active_ms, total_time_visible_ms and siblings); the matching XML attributes carry no unit in their names. Standby Bucket and Standby Reason are the high and low 16 bits of the single stored standbyBucket value; that split is applied by this parser and is not labelled in the file. Event Flags is the stored numeric 'flags' bitmask, undecoded. Reference: Google, 'UsageEvents.Event', https://developer.android.com/reference/android/app/usage/UsageEvents.Event",
                        "category": "Usage Stats"
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    }
}